CHAPTERS = [
dict(title="A Castle After Dark", scene="castle", paras=[
"Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive. Tonight we spend a long November night inside an English castle, a little over five hundred years ago. There will be no sieges and no battles. We will simply arrive at dusk, pass through the gate, and move slowly inward, room by room, while the household lays down its work and goes to sleep.",
"Make yourself comfortable, in whatever way your body likes best. Let your jaw loosen and your arms grow heavy. Breathe in slowly, and imagine cold autumn air with a trace of woodsmoke and wet leaves in it. Hold it for a moment. Then let it go, long and slow, and feel the bed take a little more of your weight.",
"You do not need to follow every word. If your thoughts wander off, let them go, and come back whenever you like. The castle will keep its own quiet hours, and if you drift away before we reach the last room, the night will go on without you, gently, exactly as it should.",
]),

dict(title="The Road Home at Dusk", scene="castle", paras=[
"We begin on a muddy lane between hedges, a mile or so from the castle, as the light goes out of the sky. The fields on either side have been ploughed and sown with winter wheat, and the furrows hold little strips of silver water from the afternoon rain. A few rooks are flying home in a loose, untidy line, calling to each other as they go.",
"Ahead of you, on a low rise above a river, the castle stands against the last of the sunset. You can see the long grey curtain wall, with round towers set along it at intervals, and the tall gatehouse in the middle, with two towers of its own pressed close together on either side of the arch. Behind the wall rises the old square keep, the oldest part of all.",
"The keep was built long ago, perhaps three or four hundred years before tonight, when the first lords came here and wanted one strong tower to live in. Over the centuries the castle grew around it, a ring of wall, then towers, then a great hall, then kitchens and chambers and a chapel, until the old tower became only one building among many, like a grandfather sitting quietly in the corner.",
"You walk on, past a mill where the wheel has been stopped for the night and the water slides over the weir with a long, steady hiss. A miller's lad is closing the shutters. A dog barks once from the yard and then thinks better of it. The smell of the river rises up, cold and green, with a little mud and a little rotting leaf in it.",
"As you come closer, you can make out the narrow windows in the towers, most of them dark, a few showing a faint glow where a candle or a fire is burning in a chamber. Thin threads of smoke rise from several chimneys, and from the louvre on the roof of the great hall, and lean slowly eastward in the breeze, until you cannot tell where the smoke ends and the dusk begins.",
"The lane turns, and now you can see the moat, a broad ribbon of still water lying around the foot of the walls. It is not a menacing thing tonight. Ducks have settled at its edge among the reeds, heads tucked under their wings, and the reflections of the towers lie upside down in it, perfectly still, with here and there a yellow window trembling on the surface.",
"High on the gatehouse a flag is stirring in the evening breeze, slowly unrolling and folding again. It carries the colours of the family who hold this castle, and when they are here, the flag flies to say so. Tonight it is flying. The lord and his household have come to stay for the winter, with their carts of bedding and chests and hangings, as they do every year after Michaelmas.",
"A great household was always on the move in those days, travelling from one house to another through the year, eating the stores of each place in turn. When they arrived, the empty rooms came alive again. Beds were set up, painted cloths were hung on the walls, the chapel was furnished with its silver and its candles, and the kitchens were lit for the first time in months.",
"Along the road behind you, a few late travellers are hurrying to reach the gate before it closes. A carter with a load of firewood, walking at his horse's head. Two women with baskets of eggs and cheese for the kitchen. A boy with a bundle of rushes on his back, bent almost double, cut from the riverbank that morning to freshen the floor of the hall.",
]),

dict(title="The Gate Is Closed", scene="bailey", paras=[
"You cross the wooden bridge over the moat, your footsteps sounding hollow on the planks, and pass under the deep arch of the gatehouse. It is like walking through a short stone tunnel. Above you, in the vaulted roof, there are narrow slots and openings, and set into the walls on either side are the grooves where the portcullis runs up and down.",
"The porter is waiting in his little lodge just inside the arch, a small room with a fire, a stool, and a window looking out onto the passage. He is an old man with a grey beard and a heavy ring of keys at his belt, and he has kept this gate for twenty years. He knows every carter and every kitchen girl by name, and he nods to each of them as they pass.",
"When the last of the travellers is through, he steps out into the passage, looks once along the empty road, and calls up to the room above. There, two men take hold of the spokes of a great wooden windlass and begin to turn it. With a slow creaking of rope and timber, the end of the drawbridge lifts from the bank and rises into the dark.",
"Then the portcullis comes down. It is a heavy grid of oak, bound and pointed with iron, and it slides down its stone grooves with a long, low rumble, until it settles onto the ground with a soft thud. The great wooden doors are swung closed behind it and barred with a beam. The castle is shut for the night, and everyone inside can feel it.",
"The porter locks the small wicket door, cut into one of the larger ones, and tests it with his hand. Then he takes the keys and carries them across the courtyard to the constable, the officer who keeps the castle in the lord's name. They will stay in the constable's chamber until morning, hanging on a nail beside his bed.",
"Now you are standing in the outer courtyard, the bailey, a wide space of beaten earth and cobbles, enclosed on every side by the walls. In daylight it is as busy as a village street. There are stables and storehouses built against the inside of the wall, a smithy with its anvil, a brewhouse, a pen of geese, and a well in the middle, with a little stone roof over its windlass.",
"Across the courtyard, a groom is leading the carter's horse to the stables, and the firewood is being stacked under a lean-to roof against the wall, log upon log, enough for a week of fires in the hall and the kitchens. The carter himself will sleep tonight in the hayloft above the horses, where the air is warm and smells of hay, and he will set off home at first light.",
"The smith has let his forge die down, and only a dull red eye of coal still shows in the hearth behind his half-open door. In the stables, the horses are pulling hay from their racks, and you can hear the steady grinding of their teeth, and every so often the soft blow of a breath through a nose. One of them puts its head out over the half-door and watches you pass.",
"Up on the wall, a dovecote is built into the corner of a tower, and the pigeons are settling for the night, murmuring to each other in their little stone holes. A cat is sitting on the edge of the well, perfectly still, looking at nothing in particular. And from somewhere near the kitchens comes a smell that makes the whole courtyard feel warmer: new bread.",
]),

dict(title="The Bakehouse and the Well", scene="bailey", paras=[
"The bakehouse is a low stone building with a tall chimney, close against the curtain wall, well away from the timber stables in case of sparks. Its door stands half open, and inside, the great domed oven glows like the mouth of a small cave. The baker is a broad, floury man in a linen apron, and his work is nearly done for the day.",
"An oven like this is heated by burning bundles of brushwood right inside it, until the stone of the dome is hot all the way through. Then the ashes are raked out, the floor is swept with a damp mop of rags on a pole, and the loaves are slid in on a long wooden peel. The heat stored in the stone does the baking, slowly falling hour by hour.",
"Tonight the last batch is coming out. The fine white loaves, made with the best flour, are for the lord's table. The darker loaves, of wheat mixed with rye, are for the household. And there are the trenchers, thick slices of coarse, stale bread, used as plates at dinner, which soak up the juices of the meat and are afterwards given away to the poor at the gate.",
"The baker's boy lifts the loaves out one by one and sets them on a long board to cool. They crackle softly as the crusts tighten. When the board is full, he covers them with a cloth, and the warm, sweet, yeasty smell drifts out through the door and across the cold courtyard, mixing with the woodsmoke and the smell of horses.",
"Then the baker begins tomorrow's bread. In a long wooden trough he mixes flour and water with a lump of yesterday's dough, saved for this very purpose, which holds the living yeast. He works it with his fists and his forearms, folding and pressing, for a long time, until it is smooth and soft. Then he covers the trough with a cloth and leaves it to rise in the warmth of the oven all night.",
"Outside, a kitchen girl comes to the well with two wooden buckets. She hooks the first one to the rope and lets it down, and the windlass turns by itself, rattling, faster and faster, until there is a faint splash far below. The well is very deep, cut down through the rock so that the castle would always have water of its own. She winds the bucket up again, slowly, hand over hand.",
"The water is so cold it seems to smoke in the evening air. She fills her second bucket, sets the yoke across her shoulders, and walks carefully back to the kitchen, not spilling a drop. The cat follows her a little way, hoping for something, and then gives up and sits down again in the doorway of the bakehouse, where the stones are warm.",
"Across the courtyard, the kitchen is closing too. The great pots have been scoured with sand and hung on their hooks. The spits have been wiped and laid on their racks. The cook, a small, brisk woman who has ruled this place since before dawn, sits for a moment on a stool by the dying fire, eats a heel of bread with a piece of cheese, and closes her eyes.",
"High above the courtyard, the sky has turned a deep, clear blue, with the first stars coming out over the battlements. A thin moon is rising beyond the gatehouse. From the great hall, across the courtyard, comes the murmur of many voices, the scrape of benches, and a burst of laughter. Supper is ending. Let us go in and find a place by the fire.",
]),

dict(title="The Great Hall After Supper", scene="hall", paras=[
"You come in through a small doorway and find yourself in a narrow passage, with a wooden screen on one side, pierced by two openings. This is the screens passage. On your other side are three doors, one to the kitchen, one to the buttery, where the ale and wine are kept, and one to the pantry, where the bread is kept. Servants are hurrying in and out of all of them with empty dishes.",
"Step through the screen, and the great hall opens up in front of you. It is long and high, like a barn made of stone, and it is the heart of the whole household. Far above, in the shadows, the roof is held up by great beams of oak, with curved braces springing from the walls, like the ribs of an upturned ship. Firelight moves softly across all of it.",
"In the very middle of the floor, on a raised square of stones and tiles, the fire is burning. There is no chimney. The smoke rises straight up into the roof and finds its way out through a louvre, a little turret of wooden slats on the ridge, which lets the smoke out and keeps the rain off. The air up there is always a little blue and hazy.",
"At the far end of the hall, on a low wooden platform called the dais, stands the high table, where the lord and lady have eaten with their guests. Behind it, on the wall, hangs a great painted cloth, with a hunting scene of hounds and a white hart running through a green forest. Tonight the lord and lady have already risen and gone up to their own rooms beyond.",
"Down in the body of the hall, the household is clearing away. The tables here are not fixed. Each is only a set of long wooden boards laid across trestles, and now the boards are being lifted off, one by one, and stacked against the wall, and the trestles folded and carried away. In a few minutes the whole long room is clear, and the floor is open from end to end.",
"The floor is covered with rushes from the river, spread thick, and mixed with sweet-smelling herbs to keep the air fresh. Tonight the boy you saw on the road is scattering his new bundle over the old, and the hall fills with the green, grassy smell of cut stems. When he treads on a sprig of dried lavender, its scent rises around him for a moment and is gone.",
"A great household might have many dozens of people in it, sometimes more than a hundred. There were officers and clerks, cooks and scullions, grooms and carters, laundresses and pages and chaplains. The great hall was where most of them ate, and where many of them gathered in the evenings, to talk, to mend things, to play at dice, and to listen to a story.",
"At this hour, a minstrel is sitting on a bench near the fire with a small harp on his knee, playing something slow and quiet, almost to himself. A few people are listening, their faces lit warm and gold from below. An old man is mending a leather strap. Two pages, no more than ten or eleven years old, are already yawning, leaning against each other on the bench.",
"Near the hearth, a big grey hound has stretched out on its side in the warmth, its legs twitching a little as it dreams of running. Another lies with its long nose on its paws, watching the fire, its eyes slowly closing and opening again. Every so often a log settles in the flames with a soft crunch, and a little drift of sparks rises up toward the dark roof.",
]),

dict(title="Compline and the Covering of the Fire", scene="hall", paras=[
"From across the courtyard, a small bell begins to ring. It is the chapel bell, calling the household to compline, the last service of the day. A few people rise from the benches and wrap their cloaks around them and go out. In the little chapel, lit by beeswax candles, the chaplain and his clerks are already standing in their places.",
"Compline is short and simple. It is sung softly, in Latin, in the half dark, and its words ask for peace through the night, and for protection from bad dreams. One of the hymns sung at this hour begins with the words Te lucis ante terminum, before the ending of the light. Then the candles are snuffed, one by one, and the chapel is left to the small red lamp that burns before the altar all night.",
"Back in the hall, the evening is coming to an end. One of the servants brings a great domed cover and sets it down over the embers of the fire, leaving only a little gap for air. The fire will smoulder slowly under it all night, safe from sparks, and in the morning it can be uncovered and blown back into flame, without needing to be lit from nothing.",
"This is the old custom of covering the fire. In French it was called couvre-feu, and from it comes our word curfew. In many towns a bell was rung at a fixed hour each evening, as a signal that fires should be covered or put out, and people should go home to rest. The story goes that William the Conqueror brought the custom to England, though more likely it was already here, and he only made people keep it.",
"Then the beds are made, right here on the floor of the hall. The lower servants, the grooms and the kitchen boys and the porters' lads, unroll their straw pallets and blankets along the walls and near the warm stones of the hearth. Some have a sack of chaff for a pillow. Others simply roll up their cloak. The hall is the warmest room in the castle, and nobody minds the company.",
"Each sleeper is given his small allowance for the night, which the household books call livery: perhaps a portion of bread and a measure of ale, so that no one should wake hungry or thirsty in the dark. You can see them settling down, taking a last mouthful, pulling their blankets up to their chins, turning onto their sides, and growing still.",
"In the chambers above the hall, the gentlemen and officers of the household are going to bed too, two or three to a room, and often two to a bed, which was thought perfectly ordinary. They hang their belts on pegs, fold their gowns over a wooden pole, and climb in under the blankets, talking quietly for a few minutes, until one of them stops answering.",
"The minstrel has put away his harp in its bag and lain down on a bench, with his hood pulled over his eyes. The two pages have been taken off to their own corner by an older servant. Someone pulls the curtain across the screens passage, to keep out the draught. The rushlights are put out, one after another, until the only light is the faint red glow seeping from under the fire cover.",
"In the dimness, the great roof seems to grow even higher, the beams fading up into darkness. The smoke from the covered fire rises thin and slow, a pale thread winding up toward the louvre. Someone is already snoring softly. A hound sighs and shifts. A log ticks as it cools. And the long hall, which was so full of noise an hour ago, becomes as quiet as a church.",
]),

dict(title="The Watch on the Walls", scene="wallwalk", paras=[
"Let us climb now, up a narrow spiral stair in the thickness of a tower wall, round and round in the dark, with one hand on the cold, worn stone of the central pillar. The steps are hollowed in the middle by centuries of feet. Here and there a slit window lets in a blade of moonlight and a breath of cold air. And then a little door, and you step out onto the top of the wall.",
"You are standing on the wall walk, a stone path running along the top of the curtain wall, from tower to tower, all the way around the castle. On the outer side rise the battlements, a row of solid stone teeth with gaps between them, so that a watcher can look out and still have shelter. The stone is wet with dew and shining faintly in the moonlight.",
"In the corner of a tower, a little iron brazier is glowing, and beside it, wrapped in a thick woollen cloak, stands the night watchman. In a great household of this time he was called the wayte. He has a pipe hanging on a cord around his neck, a lantern at his feet, and a long staff in his hand, and he has been awake since the gate was closed.",
"His duties were written down in the household books of the time, which set out how the king's own court should be run. On winter nights, they say, from Michaelmas until Holy Thursday before Easter, the wayte should pipe the watch four times through the night, and three times on the shorter nights of summer. In this castle, the steward has borrowed the same good custom.",
"The books also say he must keep a careful watch at every chamber door and every office, against fire, and against thieves, and against any other danger. So every so often he takes up his lantern and walks the length of the wall, and then goes down into the courtyard and makes his round, listening at doors, checking that the fires are covered and the candles out.",
"And for this he was given his reward: he ate in the hall with the minstrels, and at night he was allowed his livery, half a loaf and half a gallon of ale, to keep him going through the long hours. On summer nights the list adds a couple of candles, and some coal for his brazier. It was not a grand position, but it was a trusted one.",
"Now he lifts the pipe to his lips and plays a few slow notes, clear and high, which float out over the sleeping courtyard and the dark roofs and away over the moat. It is not an alarm. It is a reassurance. It says that the hour has come round, that someone is awake and watching, and that all is well. Then he lowers the pipe and is silent again.",
"From up here you can see the whole countryside, silver and grey under the moon. The river curves away between its willows, with a little mist lying on it. Beyond the river there is a village, a cluster of dark thatched roofs around a church with a square stone tower, and one tiny window still lit, where perhaps someone is sitting up with a sick child, or a cow is calving in the barn.",
"Far away, on the edge of the sky, the low line of a wooded hill. Nearer, the strips of the open fields, the dark shapes of haystacks, a windmill with its sails still. An owl is calling from the woods, a long, wavering note, and a second owl answers it from much farther off. The stars are very bright and very many, and they turn slowly, all together, over the towers.",
"The watchman pulls his cloak tighter, warms his hands at the brazier for a moment, and then sets off along the wall walk with his lantern swinging, a small gold light moving steadily away from you between the dark stone teeth of the battlements, until it reaches the next tower and turns the corner and is gone.",
]),

dict(title="The Lady's Chamber", scene="solar", paras=[
"Now we go inward one last time, beyond the dais at the end of the great hall, through a door and up a short stair, to the private rooms of the family. Here the walls are plastered and painted, a soft red with a pattern of little white flowers, and the floors are of wooden boards, laid with rush matting, warm and quiet under your feet.",
"This is the lady's chamber. It is not large, but it is the most comfortable room in the castle. A small fire is burning in a hooded fireplace set into the wall, with a proper chimney of its own, so there is no smoke in the air here at all. It throws a soft, moving light across the floor, and the room smells faintly of woodsmoke and of dried rose petals.",
"Against the far wall stands the great bed. It is the most precious piece of furniture in the whole house, worth more than all the benches and tables in the hall together. Above it hangs a canopy, called the tester, and from the canopy fall heavy curtains of dyed wool, blue with a border of embroidered vines, which can be drawn right around the bed to shut out the draughts.",
"The bed is built up in layers, like a cake. At the bottom, a mattress stuffed with straw. On top of that, a deep mattress filled with feathers. Then linen sheets, very fine and very white, which were washed and dried on the hedges and folded away with lavender. Then woollen blankets. And over everything, a coverlet lined with soft grey fur, heavy and warm.",
"At the foot of the bed stands a carved wooden chest, which holds the lady's gowns and veils, and which also serves as a seat. Low down beside the great bed, there is a little truckle bed on wheels, which can be pulled out at night and pushed back underneath in the morning. Her maid sleeps there, close by, in case she is needed in the night.",
"In the thickness of the wall there is a window, set back in a deep embrasure, with a stone bench built into each side, facing each other, so that two people can sit there and look out, or sew in the daylight, or talk quietly. Tonight the wooden shutters are half closed, and a long bar of moonlight falls through the gap and across the floor.",
"In a little niche in the wall, cut into the stone for just this purpose, a single candle is burning. It is made of beeswax, not tallow, so it burns with a clear, steady flame and a faint smell of honey. Wax candles like this were costly, and in this room they are a small luxury, the last light of the day before sleep.",
"On a cushion by the fire, a little white dog is curled up in a perfect circle, its nose tucked under its tail. Lap dogs like this were loved and spoiled in great households, and sometimes appear carved at the feet of their mistresses on tombs. It opens one eye as the fire settles, looks around the room, finds that all is well, and closes it again.",
"On the wall beside the bed hangs a woven cloth, showing a garden enclosed by a low wall, with a fountain in the middle, and trees heavy with fruit, and little birds among the flowers. When the draught from the window touches it, it sways very slightly on its rail, as if the garden itself were breathing in its sleep.",
"The lady herself is sitting on the edge of the bed while her maid unpins her veil and brushes out her long hair, slowly, stroke after stroke, the way it is done every night. They talk quietly about small things: the journey here, the cold in the chapel, a letter that must be written tomorrow. Then the veil is folded away in the chest, the lady lies down, and the curtains are drawn almost closed.",
]),

dict(title="Goodnight from the Castle", scene="solar", paras=[
"Now it is your turn to rest. Imagine that the bed curtains have been drawn almost closed around you, leaving just a narrow gap where the firelight comes through, soft and gold. The feather mattress has taken your shape. The fur coverlet is heavy and warm. There is nothing left that you need to do tonight.",
"Far below, the great hall is dark and full of slow breathing. The bread is rising quietly in its trough in the warm bakehouse. The horses are dozing on their feet in the stables. The drawbridge is up, the gate is barred, and the keys are hanging on their nail. Everything is closed, and safe, and still.",
"Let your breathing slow until it matches the deep, even breathing of the sleepers in the hall. Let your thoughts grow quiet, the way the hall grew quiet when the fire was covered. And if a thought does come, let it rise and drift away like the thin thread of smoke going up into the louvre, and out into the cold, clear night.",
"And somewhere up on the walls, the watchman is walking his round under the stars, his lantern moving slowly from tower to tower. In a little while he will lift his pipe again, and a few soft notes will drift over the roofs, to tell the sleeping castle that the hour has turned and all is well. You will not need to wake to hear them.",
"Thank you for spending this November night in the castle with The Quiet Archive. The candle is burning low in its niche, the fire is settling into embers, and the moon is shining on the moat. Sleep well. Goodnight.",
]),
]
